ns prologos::core::gen

;; ========================================
;; Gen — type-directed value generation
;; ========================================
;;
;; The gate for Spec System Phase 2's remaining half: property checking for
;; `:properties` and trait `:laws`. A law like Eq's
;;
;;   :name "reflexive"  :forall {x : A}  :holds [eq? x x]
;;
;; is stored fully structured already; what was missing is any way to produce
;; the `x`. `Gen` is that.
;;
;; ⚠ SEEDED, NOT RANDOM, and deliberately. `gen` is a pure function of a seed,
;; so a law failure is REPRODUCIBLE from the seed alone — no capturing and
;; replaying a random state, and no test that fails once a week. The caller
;; varies the seed to vary the sample; the generator itself never has a hidden
;; state to get out of sync.
;;
;; The distributions are intentionally small and boring (a bounded window
;; around zero, both signs where the type allows). Laws are falsified by
;; ordinary values far more often than by extreme ones, and a wide range would
;; mostly buy slow arithmetic. Edge cases belong in `:examples`, which check
;; specific values and already run.

exports Gen gen

trait Gen {A}
  gen : Int -> A
  :doc "Produce a value of type A from a seed. Pure: the same seed always gives the same value."

;; --- Int: a window straddling zero, so sign-sensitive laws get both ---
impl Gen Int
  defn gen [s : Int] <Int>
    int- [int-mod [int-abs s] 200] 100

;; --- Nat: NOT PROVIDED, and the reason is a real gap ---
;;
;; There is no Int -> Nat conversion anywhere in the tower. `from-nat` goes the
;; other way (Nat -> Int), `conversions.prologos` has `impl ToInt Nat` and no
;; inverse, and no `to-nat` / `int-to-nat` exists. So a seeded Nat generator has
;; nothing to build on short of counting `suc` from `zero`, which is O(n) in the
;; value and absurd for this purpose.
;;
;; Worth stating plainly because Nat is the type trait laws mention most (Eq,
;; Ord and the numeric traits all have Nat instances), so this is the first
;; thing the law checker will want. The fix is a partial `Int -> Option Nat` (or
;; a `Nat`-returning one on the absolute value) in `conversions.prologos`,
;; which is a numeric-tower decision rather than something to bolt on here.

;; --- Bool: alternates on consecutive seeds, so a caller stepping the seed
;;     by one gets both values rather than a run of one ---
impl Gen Bool
  defn gen [s : Int] <Bool>
    int-eq 0 [int-mod [int-abs s] 2]

;; --- ⚠ THE BARE `gen` WRAPPER DOES NOT EXIST, AND CANNOT YET ---------------
;;
;; `[gen 5]` is `Unbound variable` — inside this module as well as outside.
;; That is not a wiring mistake; it is the derive rule working correctly.
;;
;; `derivable-method?` (macros.rkt) derives a bare wrapper only when EVERY trait
;; parameter appears in a DOMAIN position, because the instance is chosen by
;; unifying the type var against the arguments. `gen : Int -> A` has `A` in the
;; RESULT position only, so there is nothing to unify — the exact shape
;; DEFERRED.md § "Numerics N6d-i follow-ups" item 3 describes as
;; "output-position-only methods (`from-integer : Int -> A`)".
;;
;; So `Gen` is gated on that item: choosing the instance from the EXPECTED type
;; (`def a : Int := [gen 5]`) needs the checking-mode constraint resolution item
;; 3 calls UNPROVEN at HEAD. Worth stating loudly because it means Spec Phase
;; 2's property checking — which needs `Gen` — inherits that blocker, and
;; nothing had connected the two.
;;
;; What DOES work today, and what the law checker can use in the meantime, is
;; the dictionary method: `[Int--Gen--gen 5]`, `[Bool--Gen--gen 4]`. The
;; instances below are real and tested through that route; only the bare-name
;; ergonomics are blocked.
